breaking news from the so-called gig  economy and the supreme court  has ruled that drivers do work for uber  the tech giant's appeal has been  dismissed by the highest court in the  land  and it means that drivers should be  classed as workers  and not third-party independent  contractors well it means that they  should  then get basic employment rights like  paid holidays  the judgment is likely to have big  implications for tens of thousands of  drivers and indeed  people working in other gig sectors as  well well let's bring in  skies technology correspondent roland  manthorp  morning roland just remind us how this  ended up in the supreme court  morning well it ended up through this  absolutely epic struggle  which all began way back in 2016  when two drivers james farah and yasim  aslam  brought a case to the to an employment  tribunal  claiming that they were workers now they  were both uber drivers and it's  important to note there's a subtle  distinction here that's really important  british law has three categories of  employment  full employment self-employment  and worker status which is somewhere in  the middle  uber said the drivers were fully  self-employed  the driver said they had some rights but  could retain  some flexibility those drivers they won  in the employment tribunal  they won on appeal they won an appeal  again and again and finally  it got all the way to the supreme court  and what we've just had  is an absolutely sweeping milestone  judgment that i think will have  deep significance throughout the gig  economy  because the the supreme court justices  didn't  just unanimously rule against uber and  in favor of the drivers  finding that they are indeed workers  they also push this judgment  forward right across uber's business  model  there was always a question in this  judgment  whether the judges would look very  narrowly or what was going on  back in 2016 all that long time ago  when the case was originally brought and  uber has been saying for some time well  yes  okay maybe we did we weren't perfect in  2016 but we've  changed a lot since then we do things so  differently now  we're really unrecognizable from the  company that we were then reading  we only we don't have the full details  yet but from the judgment  that was just read out the judges seem  to say that the very  way that uber operates the heart of its  business model  means that its drivers are workers  this is a worst case scenario for uber  this will have  deep ramifications not just for them but  right across the gig economy  you
 

